ITEM S4-4 — Item name matching: exact match first + pass inventory names to AI
Files:
  - AITest/Views/SaleEntryReviewView.swift (autoResolveRows)
  - AITest/Services/AIInventoryService.swift (parse functions + prompts)
  - AITest/Views/SmartSalesTextView.swift
  - AITest/Views/SmartSalesVoiceView.swift
  - AITest/Views/SmartSalesPhotoView.swift
  - AITest/Views/SmartSalesCSVView.swift
  - AITest/Views/SmartSalesPDFView.swift

Problem (two parts):
  1. autoResolveRows() only tries substring matching and requires exactly 1 candidate. If the user has "Apple" and "Apple Juice", neither matches. Also no priority for exact names.
  2. AI parse prompts don't know the inventory, so the AI returns whatever name it hears/reads ("apples", "red apple") rather than the canonical inventory name ("Apple").

Part A — Fix autoResolveRows() in SaleEntryReviewView.swift:
  private func autoResolveRows() {
      for index in rows.indices where rows[index].resolvedItem == nil {
          let query = rows[index].itemName.lowercased().trimmingCharacters(in: .whitespacesAndNewlines)
          guard !query.isEmpty else { continue }

          // 1. Exact match (case-insensitive) — highest confidence
          if let exact = allItems.first(where: { $0.name.lowercased() == query }) {
              rows[index].resolvedItem = exact
              continue
          }

          // 2. Substring match — both directions, require 3+ chars to avoid noise
          guard query.count >= 3 else { continue }
          let candidates = allItems.filter {
              $0.name.lowercased().contains(query) || query.contains($0.name.lowercased())
          }
          if candidates.count == 1 {
              rows[index].resolvedItem = candidates.first
          }
      }
  }

Part B — Pass inventory names to AI prompts:
Add @Query to each Smart Sales view to fetch all item names:
  @Query(sort: \InventoryItem.name) private var allItems: [InventoryItem]

Add a knownItemNames parameter to each AIInventoryService parse function:
  func parseSalesText(text: String, knownItemNames: [String]) async throws -> [ParsedSaleRow]
  func parseSalesTranscript(transcript: String, knownItemNames: [String]) async throws -> [ParsedSaleRow]
  func parseSalesImage(imageData: Data, knownItemNames: [String]) async throws -> [ParsedSaleRow]
  func parseSalesPDF(pages: [UIImage], knownItemNames: [String]) async throws -> [ParsedSaleRow]
  func parseSalesCSV(rows: [[String]], knownItemNames: [String]) async throws -> [ParsedSaleRow]

Prepend this to each prompt (before the user input):
  let inventoryContext = knownItemNames.isEmpty ? "" : """
  Inventory items available: \(knownItemNames.joined(separator: ", ")).
  Use the EXACT inventory name (case-sensitive) when the item mentioned matches one of these. Only fall back to the spoken/written name if no inventory item matches.

  """

Update call sites in each view:
  parsedRows = try await AIInventoryService.shared.parseSalesText(
      text: inputText,
      knownItemNames: allItems.map { $0.name }
  )
  // (similarly for voice, photo, csv, pdf)

CRITICAL RULE: This same exact-match improvement (Part A) should also be applied to the auto-resolve logic in PurchaseReviewView if it has its own autoResolve function — check and apply the same pattern.

Also check VoiceInventoryView, ImageInventoryView, PaperInventoryView (SmartCount views) — if they have a similar autoResolve step, apply the exact match priority fix there too.

